An intimate wedding brings together 30 to 60 guests instead of 150, and it is not a wedding on the cheap: it is a different format, with rules of its own. At Château de la Huberdière, near Amboise, we host both, and the small weddings are the ones couples still talk to us about years later.

This guide covers what you need to settle before you start: guest numbers, the real budget, where your close family sleeps, how the day runs, and what this format does not allow.

## What is an intimate wedding?

An intimate wedding is a deliberately small reception, between 30 and 60 guests, where you invite only the people who genuinely count. The vocabulary has grown over the past few years: below 30 guests it is called a micro wedding, and under ten people an elopement, that near-private ceremony held far from home.

Out of the roughly 240,000 weddings celebrated in France each year according to INSEE, the share of small formats climbed after 2020 and never came back down. The word sounds modern, the practice is old: our grandparents married at forty, in the family house, and nobody called it a trend.

## How many guests for an intimate wedding?

Between 30 and 60 guests, with a practical ceiling around 50: beyond that you fall back into the logistics of a large wedding, endless seating plan and sound system included. Below 30, the mood shifts again and becomes that of a long family dinner.

Château de la Huberdière hosts 30 to 150 guests. An intimate wedding therefore never has to look for space here, and that is exactly what makes the format comfortable: you are not trying to fill a room built for twice your number.

## What budget for an intimate wedding?

A small wedding costs less in total, but more per guest, and anyone telling you otherwise is doing the maths badly. Exclusive hire of an estate is a fixed cost: it does not drop because you invite fewer people. At La Huberdière it runs from 4,000 to 8,000 € depending on the season for a Friday to Sunday weekend, with all 21 beds included.

Catering, by contrast, follows guest numbers, between 80 and 180 € per head. That is where the small format pays off: at 40 guests, the single heaviest line of any wedding falls below 7,200 €, when it passes 12,000 € at a hundred. You get that margin back elsewhere, on the food or on the photographer.

Two lines are worth negotiating anywhere. Furniture, which we charge at 15 € per person to save you an outside rental, and drinks. We charge no corkage at all, so you bring your own wines and champagnes. The full breakdown sits in our guide to the [cost of a château wedding in the Loire](/en/blog/chateau-wedding-cost).

## One table, one dinner

At 40 guests, everyone fits in the same room and often around the same table, and that is the real benefit of an intimate wedding. Speeches carry without a microphone. Nobody ends up at the far table, the one the couple only reaches over coffee.

![Long table dressed in white in a wood-panelled room, a dozen wedding guests around candles and flowers, doors open onto the grounds](/images/inline/restauration/restauration-06.webp "One table of twelve to forty covers: the format does away with a fifteen-table seating plan and the sound system that comes with it.")

It changes the work in the kitchen too. Lodovica cooks Italian, with produce from the market and the kitchen garden, and at forty covers she can plate every dish where a hundred and fifty would force a buffet. Dietary requirements are taken into account without anyone having to mention them three times. Our page on [dining at the château](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) sets out the evening menus.

## Where do guests sleep at a small wedding?

The château sleeps 21 people in 10 bedrooms, all included in the weekend hire, which covers most of an intimate wedding. At a reception of 40 guests, half of them stay on site, starting with parents, witnesses and anyone travelling a long way.

The rest find rooms in Amboise or Nazelles-Négron, a few minutes away by car. The advantage is real and rarely costed: nobody gets back in a car at two in the morning, and the party does not end with the last train. The next morning breakfast stretches out, and it is often the best moment of the weekend. Our guide to [renting a château with friends and family](/en/blog/rent-chateau-group-weekend) goes further into the full-weekend format.

## What spaces suit 30 to 60 people?

A small format does not need a room for two hundred covers, it needs several rooms on a human scale that a group can move between. The vaulted hall takes the dinner, the four drawing rooms serve for the drinks reception and the quiet moments, the Orangery and the terrace open onto the grounds.

![Polished wooden staircase and wall of books in a château drawing room, herringbone parquet floor and wrought-iron chandelier](/images/inline/salons-interieurs/salons-interieurs-09.webp "The drawing rooms work as wet-weather back-up, cloakroom and conversation corner. At forty guests, a group fills the whole ground floor without ever getting lost in it.")

The humanist ceremony takes place outside, under the century-old trees of the fourteen hectare grounds, or on the terrace facing the hydrangeas. If it rains, it moves into the drawing rooms, thirty seconds away on foot. The whole estate is yours, with no other event running alongside: forty people across fourteen hectares really are on their own.

## How do you cut the guest list without falling out?

The list is the only genuinely hard part of an intimate wedding, and it is settled with one rule announced early, never case by case. Set the principle before you write down a single name: family as far as first cousins, friends you have actually seen this year, no colleagues, no unknown plus-ones.

A clear rule can be defended in one sentence and hurts nobody, because it targets nobody. The couples who suffer are the ones who carve up the list guest by guest over six months. Say it on the invitation: "we are marrying at forty" is perfectly well understood, and puts an end to the negotiating.

## Which date suits a small wedding?

A small format frees up the date, and that is its most underrated advantage: at forty guests, a Friday in October is as easy to arrange as a Saturday in June, and costs a good deal less. Getting a hundred and fifty people together on a working day is impossible; getting forty is not.

![The château grounds in autumn, yellow and red foliage, cypress trees and tall grasses under a grey sky](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-11.webp "In mid-October the grounds turn russet for about three weeks. It is also when the hire rate is at its lowest.")

We are open all year. Autumn and winter offer the same setting at a lower hire rate, and a light that comes in low between the tall trees around four in the afternoon. If the cold season appeals, our guide to a [winter wedding in a Loire château](/en/blog/winter-wedding-chateau-loire) covers how a heated venue works.

## What an intimate wedding does not allow

A wedding of forty cannot please everyone, and that is the price of the format, to be accepted from the start. Some relatives will take it badly, whatever rule you set.

Two more practical limits are worth knowing. Some caterers apply a minimum number of covers, often fifty, which you should check before signing. And economies of scale work against you: the photographer, the DJ and the flowers cost the same for forty as for a hundred and twenty.

Finally, La Huberdière has no spa and no room for three hundred covers, and we would rather say so before the visit. This is a sixteenth-century family home, 2 hrs 20 from Paris and 30 minutes from Tours: for a wedding of a hundred and fifty it works, for a wedding of forty it was made for the job.

